    "busprobe.stage.askStart":
      "The interface asks the core to send a request and wait for a reply. The core creates a correlation_id — it will be used later to find the waiter. From this moment the command thread sleeps.",
    "busprobe.stage.routeDecode":
      "The core turns the JSON from the interface into a typed Rust message. A parse error would break the chain right here — the queue would never be reached.",
    "busprobe.stage.queueEnqueue":
      "The message is put on the queue. A separate dispatcher thread will pick it up: this is where the path splits in time and asynchrony appears.",
    "busprobe.stage.dispatchBegin":
      "The dispatcher has taken the message. The difference from the previous stage is the time it spent in the queue. That is the price of asynchronous delivery.",
    "busprobe.stage.handlerInvoke":
      "The dispatcher calls the event subscribers. There may be several; if one of them panics, the others still receive the message.",
    "busprobe.stage.publishReply":
      "The handler publishes the reply — an ordinary event with the same correlation_id. Here it publishes from a separate thread after a delay, so the dispatcher is free while waiting.",
    "busprobe.stage.correlationMatch":
      "The core checks whether anyone is waiting for an event with this identifier. On a request it misses: someone waits for a reply, not for a request. That is why ask needs an explicit reply_topic.",
    "busprobe.stage.sinkEmit":
      "The event goes to the interface. Here the chain leaves Rust: from now on Vue subscribers and the journal see it.",
    "busprobe.stage.askReply":
      "The waiter has woken up and returned the value to the interface. The command thread runs again — with the same value that the reply event brought.",

